    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/postdoctoral-researcher-global-health-economics-beacon-hd": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Global Health Economics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Global Health Economics – BEACON-HD | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Postdoctoral Researcher in Global Health Economics – BEACON-HD Listen Published Tuesday 8 Sep 2026 Deadline Friday 9 Oct 2026 Work area Academic staff Organisational unit Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) Salary € 4.925 - € 6.682 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Introduction BEACON-HD is an international, Horizon Europe-funded research project testing two promising strategies — telemedicine with self-monitoring and community-based medication delivery — to strengthen primary health care systems and improve the management of hypertension and type 2 diabetes in rural Rwanda and Uganda. As a postdoctoral researcher, you will play a leading role in the economic and equity analyses within this large-scale field experiment, working closely with an international consortium of health economists, clinicians and implementation scientists. Join us to help generate the evidence needed to make chronic disease care more effective, efficient and fair in low-resource settings. Job description Are you interested in leading applied research within a large-scale field experiment aimed at improving the management of chronic disease in low-resource settings? Do you want to help make healthcare delivery more effective, efficient and equitable for people living with hypertension and diabetes in Sub-Saharan Africa? We offer a post-Doc opportunity for 36 months (90% research, 10% teaching) within BEACON-HD, a large EU Horizon Europe-funded international consortium project. BEACON-HD is testing two promising implementation strategies, telemedicine with self-monitoring and community-based medication delivery, through a large-scale field experiment in rural Rwanda and Uganda. The project combines this field experiment with qualitative, behavioral and economic research to understand not only whether these strategies work, but how, for whom, and at what cost. As a postdoctoral researcher, you will take a leading role in the economic and equity work package, working closely with the project's PI and the wider consortium. Your responsibilities will include: Contributing to and helping coordinate the BEACON-HD field experiment, from the design of data collection instruments to implementation, analysis and interpretation of results, in close collaboration with consortium partners across Europe, Rwanda and Uganda. Leading the collection, quality assurance and analysis of data on costs, resource use and outcomes together with our in-country partners, including field visits to Rwanda and/or Uganda. Building and extending a decision-analytic (Markov) model to estimate the cost-effectiveness of telemedicine with self-monitoring and community medication delivery, relative to standard care, from the perspective of the public health systems in both countries. Leading a budget-impact analysis assessing the short- and medium-term financial implications of scaling up these strategies. Leading an equity-modelling study evaluating how the implementation strategies affect health outcomes across socioeconomic subgroups, and their potential to contribute to universal health coverage. Co-authoring peer-reviewed publications and presenting findings at international conferences. Contributing to the day-to-day coordination of the work package This postdoc position combines field-based data collection and analysis with decision-analytic modelling and applied econometric work. It offers the opportunity to generate new methodological and empirical insights into the evaluation of health-system-strengthening strategies, helping to guide fairer and more efficient healthcare policy in low- and middle-income countries. Job requirements We are looking for a highly motivated and talented candidate with the following qualifications: Must-haves: A completed PhD (or a PhD close to completion) in health economics, development economics, economics, econometrics, or a closely related discipline. Strong analytical and quantitative skills, with knowledge of economic evaluation methods such as decision-analytic or cost-effectiveness modelling. Proficiency in statistical programming (e.g., Stata, R). Excellent written and spoken English, with the ability to communicate complex ideas clearly. Ability to work independently on a research agenda while collaborating within a large, multidisciplinary, international consortium. Willingness to travel to Rwanda and Uganda for fieldwork and consortium meetings. Nice-to-haves: Demonstrated experience conducting research in development economics or health economics. Hands-on experience designing and/or implementing fieldwork or field experiments, ideally in low- and middle-income country settings. Familiarity with Markov modelling, budget-impact analysis, or equity-weighted economic evaluation. Prior research experience in Sub-Saharan Africa or other global health / global development contexts. Experience working with trial or field-experiment data. Interest in contributing to broader project tasks beyond one's own work package, within a large international consortium. A track record of, or clear potential for, publishing in high-impact peer-reviewed journals. Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and varied job in an enthusiastic team, with excellent working conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU). The start date of this position is 1 January 2027 and you will be based at the department of Applied Economics at Erasmus School of Economics (ESE). This position is for 1,0 fte. The salary is dependent on your experience and knowledge and ranges from a minimum of € 3.706 (scale 10) to a maximum of € 6.682 gross per month (scale 11) on a fulltime basis (38 hours), in accordance with the CAO-NU. This postdoc position offers a total duration of 3 years. Everything else we offer you; you can find below! Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 41 days of paid leave with a 40 hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre ! Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR , FAME or QuEUR. Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget and free access to our university library . Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers fully paid birth leave, partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare (daycare not paid for by EUR). Moving to the Netherlands for your job with EUR? Then you may be eligible for the Expat-ruling if you meet the requirements of the Belastingdienst (Dutch tax agency), and with our Dual Career Programme we will also help your partner find the right job for them. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) is a leading school in economics in the Netherlands. It has a strong international reputation and aims to do outstanding research. Moreover, it provides excellent education at the BA., MA. and Ph.D. levels. Much research at Erasmus School of Economics is policy relevant, has social impact, and offers a strong research culture, ample research resources, and comparatively low teaching loads. www.eur.nl/en/ese Group The Health Economics research group is part of the Department of Applied Economics, together with research groups in strategy and behavioral economics. The Department offers a vibrant research environment with a focus on societally relevant economic questions ( www.eur.nl/en/ese/department-applied-economics ). Its researchers are members of the Tinbergen Institute, one of Europe's leading research institutes and graduate schools in economics, econometrics and finance ( www.tinbergen.nl ). The group's research interests include behavioral and experimental health economics, global health economics, and health and labor economics. Through EsCHER, one of Europe's largest health economics centers, and a jointly organized seminar series, you will have close ties with health economists at the Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management (ESHPM). Department The Health Economics group is part of the Department of Applied Economics together with research groups in strategy and behavioral economics. The Department offers a vibrant research environment with a focus on societally relevant economic questions ( www.eur.nl/en/ese/department-applied-economics ). Its researchers are members of the Tinbergen Institute, one of Europe's leading research institutes and graduate schools in economics, econometrics and finance ( www.tinbergen.nl ). It is also part of the Erasmus Research Institute in Management ( www.erim.eur.nl ), where its members cooperate with scholars of the Rotterdam School of Management (RSM), one of the leading business schools worldwide. Additional information More information For questions regarding the research project please contact the project supervisor: dr. Carlos Riumalló-Herl riumalloherl@ese.eur.nl . For questions regarding the application procedure please contact Linda Klaver (Operations Manager) at om.ae@ese.eur.nl Applying Please submit your complete application before 9 October 2026 . Applications will be reviewed by the recruitment committee during the complete time this vacancy is online. We look forward to receiving your application! Persons of all gender identities or expressions, sexual orientations, religions, ethnicities, ages, neurodiversities, functional impairments, citizenships, or any other aspect are welcome to apply and join the EUR community. Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Postdoctoral Researcher in Global Health Economics – BEACON-HD Close Interested? Interested? Compare @count study programme Close Compare @count study programme @title Duration: @duration Remove @title Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Postdoctoral Researcher in Global Health Economics – BEACON-HD Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/data-support-lead-faculty-liaison": {
      "title": "Data Support Lead & Faculty Liaison",
      "description": "Data Support Lead & Faculty Liaison | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Featured results Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Data Support Lead & Faculty Liaison Listen Published Monday 7 Sep 2026 Deadline Sunday 27 Sep 2026 Work area Management Organisational unit University Library (UB) Salary € 4.925 - € 6.682 Employment 0,8 fte - 1 fte Introduction Erasmus University Rotterdam Library is looking for a driven, academically grounded professional to take on a dual role: Team Lead and Faculty Liaison within the Data Team of Library Research Support. You will be the bridge between academic research and library services. As Faculty Liaison, you are the primary contact and face of the University Library for the faculties of ESHPM (Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management) and ESSB (Erasmus School of Social and Behavioural Sciences). As Team Lead, you will coordinate the Data Support Team and shape the strategic development of open research infrastructure by exploring and implementing sustainable, digitally autonomous solutions. This role is for someone who is at home in the academic world, understands how researchers think and work, and who does not just understand Open Science but wants to actively shape it. Job description As Faculty Liaison You serve as the primary point of contact for academic and support staff within ESHPM and ESSB - Pedagogical Sciences. You maintain and strengthen relationships between the University Library and both faculties, proactively identifying developments in research and education. You are responsible for acquisition of scholarly content, maintain contacts with publishers and manage specific content budgets. You contribute to University Library-wide projects and collaborate closely with fellow Faculty Liaisons. As Data Support Lead You coordinate and lead the Data Support team, which consists of ten professionals with diverse areas of expertise, facilitating effective collaboration. You support your team members in their personal and professional development, including through development conversations. You promote effective collaboration with the research data community (within the team, the University Library, Erasmus University Rotterdam, and at a national level). You keep up to date with developments in the field of research data and research data infrastructures, and translate these into high-quality services. You guide the implementation of the team's annual, ensuring alignment with the strategic priorities of the University Library, and contribute to the development of future strategies and plans for the team. You contribute to strategic workforce planning. Job requirements A master's degree, preferably in a social or health science–related discipline. Knowledge of and experience with Open Science, open data, and open research infrastructure. Experience working in or with academia — as a researcher, information specialist, or in a comparable role. Experience conducting advanced literature research. Experience in a leadership role with responsibility for HR-related tasks, such as staff support, performance appraisal, and professional development. Excellent communication skills in English; willingness to develop Dutch to C1 level. A collaborative personality that builds bridges across disciplines, teams, and organisational levels. Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and varied job in an enthusiastic team, with excellent working conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU). The start date of this position will be agreed upon and you will be based at Campus Woudestein - Rotterdam at the University Library (UL). This position is for 0.8-1.0 FTE. The salary is dependent on your experience and knowledge and ranges from a minimum of € 4,925 to a maximum of € 6,682 gross per month (scale 11) on a fulltime basis (38 hours), in accordance with the CAO-NU. The contract is offered for a period of 1 year and can be converted into an employment contract for an indefinite period if suitability is proven and if continued in the same position, if (financial and/or organizational) circumstances remain the same. Everything else we offer you; you can find below! - Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 41 days of paid leave with a 40 hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre Opens external ! - Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR Opens external , FAME or QuEUR. - Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget and free access to our university library . - Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. - Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare (daycare not paid for by EUR). - Moving to the Netherlands for your job with EUR? Then you may be eligible for the 30%-ruling if you meet the requirements of the Belastingdienst (Dutch tax agency), and with our Dual Career Programme we will also help your partner find the right job for them. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service The University Library As the content manager of Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR), the University Library (UL) supports students, lecturers and researchers in creating, sharing and deepening knowledge. All services of the University Library are openly accessible. In this way, we contribute to a positive societal impact—not only within the academic community, but beyond it as well. The University Library has an open culture in which collaboration, working on the basis of trust, and taking responsibility are key values. Additional information Are you enthusiastic, but would like to ask some questions about the position first? Then you can contact Matthijs Holwerda, Head (interim) of Library Research Support,via holwerda@eur.nl . For questions about the procedure, you can contact our recruiter Iris Konijnendijk at iris.konijnendijk@eur.nl . Applying We look forward to receiving your application for the role of Data Support Lead & Faculty Liaison. The University Library uses an anonymous application procedure . We attach great importance to diversity and believe it is essential that characteristics such as age, background and gender do not play any role in the selection process. How does the anonymous application procedure work? We kindly ask you to submit three documents: A cv containing only your initials A motivation letter containing only your initials A separate document with your personal details Anonymising the cv and motivation letter means that names, age, gender (and any other personal identifying information) are omitted. After the initial selection, candidates are invited for an introductory interview. If this meeting is mutually positive, a second, more in-depth interview will follow, for which you will be asked to prepare a sample case. After the second interview, we will select the final candidate. We look forward to receiving your application! Persons of all gender identities or expressions, sexual orientations, religions, ethnicities, ages, neurodiversities, functional impairments, citizenships, or any other aspect are welcome to apply and join the EUR community. Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Job alert Please select your preferences. When a new job becomes available that matches these preference we'll send you an email. Fields marked with a * are required. First Name Last Name Email address Password Your password requires a minimum of 8 characters of which 2 non-alphabetic Confirm password Business Unit Any Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) Erasmus School of Law (ESL) Erasmus School of History, Culture and Communication (ESHCC) Erasmus School of Social and Behavioural Sciences (ESSB) Erasmus School of Philosophy (ESPhil) Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) International Institute of Social Studies (ISS) Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management (ESHPM) General Management Directorate (ABD) University Library (UB) Convergence Human Resources (HR) Finance (FIN) Education and Student Affairs (E&S) Erasmus Digitalisation and Information Services (EDIS) Real Estate & Facilities (RE&F) Engagement & Research Services (ERS) Marketing & Communications (M&C) RSM BV Institute for Housing and Urban Development Studies (IHS) Any Any Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) Erasmus School of Law (ESL) Erasmus School of History, Culture and Communication (ESHCC) Erasmus School of Social and Behavioural Sciences (ESSB) Erasmus School of Philosophy (ESPhil) Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) International Institute of Social Studies (ISS) Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management (ESHPM) General Management Directorate (ABD) University Library (UB) Convergence Human Resources (HR) Finance (FIN) Education and Student Affairs (E&S) Erasmus Digitalisation and Information Services (EDIS) Real Estate & Facilities (RE&F) Engagement & Research Services (ERS) Marketing & Communications (M&C) RSM BV Institute for Housing and Urban Development Studies (IHS) OK Cancel Expertise Any Administration & Secretariat Policy & Advice IT Marketing & Communication Human Resources Finance Legal Real Estate & Facilities Management Education Research, development, innovation Academic staff PhD Procurement Any Any Administration & Secretariat Policy & Advice IT Marketing & Communication Human Resources Finance Legal Real Estate & Facilities Management Education Research, 